#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197971 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a116e0d-3309-5148-a0ba-16993bc0deb7" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral Given a Diagram"}] :question/id #uuid "fe87a57b-09fc-5c98-9d4c-e3ba3b95c2a9" :question/math-academy-id "e-12419" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle 0, \\, x, \\, -z^2 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that represents the boundary of the solid shown above. Let $D$ be the projection of the solid onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A$.\nDetermine the missing expression.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4144/Source/Images/020906e1438cc7bd21000dc9fa4ca331.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z = g(x,y) = 0$ is oriented upward, and\n- the lower surface $S_2: z = h(x,y) = -\\sqrt{4 - x^2 - y^2}$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper plane $S_1$. Since the positive orientation is upward, the unit normal vector to the plane is $\\mathbf{n} = \\langle 0,0,1 \\rangle$. As a result, we have\n$∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot ndS; = ∬_(D)⟨0, x,-z^{2}⟩ \\cdot ⟨0, 0, 1⟩dA; = ∬_(D) - z^{2}dA; = ∬_(D)0dA; = 0$.\n- Consider the lower hemisphere $S_2$.We compute the partial derivatives of $z = h(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂h}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}}; \\frac{∂h}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}) = \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}}$\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n$P = 0, \\qquad Q = x, \\qquad R = -z^2 = x^2 + y^2 - 4$.\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS|= - ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y} + R)dA; = - ∬_(D)(-0 \\cdot \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} - x \\cdot \\frac{y}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} + x^{2} + y^{2} - 4)dA; = ∬_(D)\\frac{xy}{\\sqrt{4 - x^{2} - y^{2}}} - x^{2} - y^{2} + 4dA$.\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)x y 4 - x 2 - y 2 - x 2 - y 2 + 4dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186197972 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "5fb5ffe1-51cb-5db8-b0a4-effd27cc623f" :question/math-academy-id "e-12420" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $\\mathbf{F} = \\langle x, \\, y, \\, 0 \\rangle$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the parabolic cylinder $z=1-x^2$ and the paraboloid $z=x^2+y^2$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $\\mathbf{F}$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n$\\displaystyle \\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathrm{d}\\mathbf{S} = \\iint\\limits_D \\,\\bbox[3pt, border: 1pt solid black]{\\phantom{AAAAAAA}} \\: \\textrm{d}A$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?" :question/worked-solution "Since we have a closed surface, the positive orientation is outward-pointing by convention. So,\n\n- the upper surface $S_1: z=g(x,y)=1-x^2$ is oriented upward, and\n- the lower surface $S_2: z=h(x,y)=x^2+y^2$ is oriented downward.\n\nNow, we write down the flux across each of the surfaces separately.\n\n- Consider the upper parabolic cylinder $S_1$.We compute the partial derivatives of $z=g(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂g}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(1 - x^{2}) =-2x; \\frac{∂g}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(1 - x^{2}) = 0$\n\n> If we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n> $P = x, \\qquad Q = y, \\qquad R = 0$.\n> As a result, since the positive orientation is upward, we have\n> $∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS|= ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y} + R)dA; = ∬_(D)(-x \\cdot (-2x) - y \\cdot (0) + 0)dA; = ∬_(D)2x^{2}dA$.\n\n- Consider the lower paraboloid $S_2$. We compute the partial derivatives of $z=h(x,y){:}$\n$\\frac{∂h}{∂x}|= \\frac{∂}{∂x}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2x; \\frac{∂h}{∂y}|= \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x^{2} + y^{2}) = 2y$\nIf we denote $\\mathbf{F}=\\langle P, \\, Q, \\, R \\rangle$, then\n$P = x, \\qquad Q = y, \\qquad R = 0$.\nAs a result, since the positive orientation is downward, we have\n$∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS|= - ∬_(D)(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x} - Q\\frac{∂h}{∂y} + R)dA; = - ∬_(D)(-x \\cdot (2x) - y \\cdot (2y) + 0)dA; = - ∬_(D) - 2x^{2} - 2y^{2}dA; = ∬_(D)2x^{2} + 2y^{2}dA$.\n\nTherefore, the flux across the entire surface is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(S)F \\cdot dS &= ∬_(S_{1})F \\cdot dS + ∬_(S_{2})F \\cdot dS \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)2x^{2}dA + ∬_(D)2x^{2} + 2y^{2}dA \\\\\n&= ∬_(D)4 x 2 + 2 y 2dA\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4d69d3c-3bb7-5e99-b606-1bbd538fa1c4" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux as a Double Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c4fc4a51-e5cf-55f3-bf0b-d292d055df1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194190} {:answer/id #uuid "e06a40c6-d550-5843-953f-340f162a132f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2x - 3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194191} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c7d517-23f6-5ae5-a893-ce88d1568696" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194192} {:answer/id #uuid "0e3045f4-4943-5d12-8b08-ae1d109cd0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194193} {:answer/id #uuid "66917aab-4a61-553e-8478-7715ad47eacb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3x - y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194194}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0e3045f4-4943-5d12-8b08-ae1d109cd0b3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2x - 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194193} :answer-field/id #uuid "570ebdc1-1d0c-513a-a91f-ce1b245181f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194189}] :question/id #uuid "5f651353-551c-55cb-9916-e19d1d757989" :question/math-academy-id "q-161907" :question/problem "Consider the vector field $F = ⟨1, 0,-x⟩$ and the closed surface $S$ that encloses the finite region between the paraboloid $z = x^{2} + y^{2}$ and the plane $z = 1 + x$. Let $D$ be the projection of $S$ onto the $xy$-plane. The flux of $F$ across $S$ can be expressed in terms of a double integral as\n\n$∬_(S)F \\cdot dS = ∬_(D)A A A A A A AdA$.\nWhich of the following could be the missing expression?"}]}
